@@s001 Matrices and products
@@s002 dato:one row,due:one column,ok:one element
@@s003 dato:how it is read now
@@s004 dato:A,due:B,ok:result
@@s005 dato:a row of A,ok:the same, now a column
@@s006 ora:row of A,dato:components of x,ok:result
@@s007 A 2 × 2 matrix times a vector of 2 components. The result is a vector of 2 components.
@@s008 <b>Seen by rows</b>: the first component of the result is the inner product of <b>row 1</b> with x: 2·1 + 1·2 = <b>4</b>.
@@s009 Row 2 with x: 1·1 + 3·2 = <b>7</b>. Each row is a linear equation, that is an inner product with x.
@@s010 Ax = [4, 7]. "Matrix multiplication is just repeated use of the inner product": once for each row. Each output is a <b>weighted sum</b> of the inputs.
@@s011 due:columns of A,ora:weighted columns,ok:Ax
@@s012 ora:row i of A,due:column j of B,ok:element (i, j) of AB
@@s013 dato:first factor,due:second factor,ok:result,no:not possible
@@s014 ora:diagonal,dato:x,ok:result
@@s015 due:columns of Q,dato:v,ok:Qv
@@s016 Matrices and products
@@s017 A matrix is a table of numbers. In the course it has three roles: <b>holding the data</b>, representing a linear <b>transformation</b>, and writing a <b>system of equations</b> in one line (that is, learning a model). Here: the notation, the element-by-element operations, the transpose, the matrix-vector product in the two views, the product of matrices, the shapes, the identity and orthogonal matrices. Slides 28–50 of lecture 2.
@@s018 What a matrix is
@@s019 A <b>matrix</b> is a rectangular table of numbers. An <b>m × n</b> matrix ("m by n") has m rows and n columns: rows are always named first. In NumPy it is a 2D array and <code>A.shape == (m, n)</code>.
@@s020 The element in row i and column j is written <span class="m">A<sub>ij</sub></span>. In the slides and books one counts from 1; in NumPy from 0, so <span class="m">A<sub>23</sub></span> is <code>A[1, 2]</code>. Each row is a vector (horizontal), each column is a vector (vertical): a matrix is a stack of vectors, read in two directions.
@@s021 Special cases: <b>square</b> (m = n), <b>column vector</b> (n = 1), <b>row vector</b> (m = 1). A 1 × 1 matrix is a number.
@@s022 Rows, columns, elements
@@s023 Three ways to use a matrix
@@s024 Slide 29 lists the uses of matrices in the course. The table is the same, only the way of reading it changes.
@@s025 <b>Data container</b> (<i>data storage</i>): one row per example, one column per feature. The dataset of poses is an 18 × 50 matrix; a greyscale image is a matrix of pixels.
@@s026 <b>Transformation</b> (<i>linear mapping</i>): the matrix acts on vectors, <span class="m">x ↦ Ax</span>. The columns say where the vectors of the basis go: it is the viewpoint of week 4.
@@s027 <b>System of equations</b>: each row is the list of coefficients of a linear equation, and <span class="m">Ax = b</span> writes a whole system in one formula. Learning a linear model means solving such a system in the weights.
@@s028 The same A, read in three ways
@@s029 Element-by-element operations
@@s030 The simplest operations are extensions of those on vectors (slide 31) and work <b>box by box</b>.
@@s031 Operation
@@s032 Rule
@@s033 NumPy
@@s034 sum, subtraction
@@s035 element by element, same shape
@@s036 by a scalar
@@s037 each element times the number
@@s038 <b>Hadamard</b> product ⊙
@@s039 element by element (<b>not</b> the matrix product)
@@s040 <b>matrix product</b>
@@s041 rows × columns (below)
@@s042 The Hadamard product (<i>Hadamard product</i>, or <i>elementwise multiplication</i>) is used for example to apply a "mask" to an image. But it does not have the properties of the matrix product, and in NumPy a single symbol is enough to confuse them.
@@s043 A + B, 2A, A ⊙ B
@@s044 The transpose
@@s045 The <b>transpose</b> <span class="m">Aᵀ</span> is obtained by swapping rows and columns: row i of A becomes column i of Aᵀ. In formula <span class="m">(Aᵀ)<sub>ij</sub> = A<sub>ji</sub></span>, and an m × n matrix becomes n × m.
@@s046 It is needed continually to make shapes work in products. The inner product itself is written <span class="m">uᵀw</span>: a row vector (1 × n) times a column vector (n × 1) gives a number (1 × 1). In least squares <span class="m">XᵀX</span> appears.
@@s047 In NumPy <code>A.T</code>. Two details: it is a <b>view</b> (it does not copy the data, it reads them in another order, so modifying <code>A.T</code> modifies <code>A</code> too), and on a 1D array it does nothing, because a 1D vector is neither row nor column (<a href="nozioni-numpy.html">NumPy</a>).
@@s048 From 2 × 3 to 3 × 2
@@s049 Matrix times vector: the view by rows
@@s050 The product <span class="m">Ax</span> between an m × n matrix and a vector of n components gives a vector of <b>m</b> components. Component i of the result is the <b>inner product of row i of A with x</b>.
@@s051 This is "matrix multiplication is just repeated use of the inner product": the inner product is repeated once for each row. Read as a model: each output is a weighted sum of the inputs, and each row contains the weights of one output. It is the view of <b>systems of equations</b> and hyperplanes (a row = an equation = a hyperplane).
@@s052 The dimensions
@@s053 An <b>m × n</b> matrix times a vector of <b>n</b> components gives a vector of <b>m</b> components. The number of columns of A must equal the length of x. It is a function from ℝⁿ to ℝᵐ: the "non-square matrices" of the slides are transformations between different dimensions.
@@s054 A = [[2, 1], [1, 3]] for x = [1, 2], row by row
@@s055 Matrix times vector: the view by columns
@@s056 The same product can also be read by columns: <span class="m">Ax = x₁·(column 1) + x₂·(column 2) + … </span>. The components of x are the <b>weights</b> of a linear combination of the columns of A.
@@s057 This view answers the question of slide 45, "where does the input vector go?": the vector [1, 0] goes to the first column, [0, 1] to the second, and every other vector goes to the corresponding combination. It is the view of <b>transformations</b> (week 4) and of <b>bases</b> (week 6).
@@s058 An important consequence: the possible results of Ax are all and only the linear combinations of the columns. This set is called the <b>column space</b> (<i>column space</i>), and it decides whether a system Ax = b has a solution (<a href="w03-sistemi-lineari.html">linear systems</a>).
@@s059 Ax as a mixture of the columns
@@s060 Matrix times matrix
@@s061 The product <span class="m">AB</span> extends the matrix-vector product: <span class="m">(AB)<sub>ij</sub></span> is the inner product between the <b>row i of A</b> and the <b>column j of B</b>. An m × p result requires m·p inner products.
@@s062 Equivalent, and often more useful: column j of AB is <b>A multiplied by column j of B</b>. So "multiplying by B and then by A" means applying one transformation after the other: the product of matrices is the <b>composition</b> of transformations, like f(g(x)) for functions.
@@s063 (m × n) · (n × p) = m × p
@@s064 (AB)<sub>ij</sub> = <mark class="ora">row i of A</mark> · <mark class="due">column j of B</mark>
@@s065 "Matrix multiplication is just repeated use of the inner product."
@@s066 For example (AB)₁₁ = [2, 1] · [0, 1] = 1.
@@s067 column j of AB = <mark class="ok">A · (column j of B)</mark>
@@s068 Multiplying by B and then by A is one transformation after the other: the composition (week 4).
@@s069 AB element by element
@@s070 Shapes and order
@@s071 Before any product the shapes are checked: <b>(m × n)(n × p) = m × p</b>. The inner dimensions must coincide, the outer ones give the shape of the result. If the inner ones do not coincide the product does not exist, and NumPy raises <code>ValueError</code> (the full rule is in <a href="nozioni-dimensioni.html">dimensions in products</a>).
@@s072 The <b>order matters</b>. With non-square matrices AB may exist and BA not, or have different shapes. Even with square matrices, in general AB ≠ BA: the product is not commutative. It remains associative, (AB)C = A(BC), and distributive, A(B + C) = AB + AC.
@@s073 Shapes in NumPy
@@s074 The transpose swaps the two dimensions.
@@s075 The "inner" dimensions must coincide; the outer ones give the shape of the result. <code>D @ D.T</code> contains all the inner products between rows: it is the basis of covariance and similarities.
@@s076 Inner dimensions different (3 and 2): NumPy raises <code>ValueError</code>. The first check in case of error is always <code>.shape</code>.
@@s077 Inner dimensions equal, outer ones give the result
@@s078 Identity and diagonal matrices
@@s079 The <b>identity matrix</b> <span class="m">I</span> is square, with 1 on the main diagonal and 0 elsewhere. It changes nothing: <span class="m">Ix = x</span> and <span class="m">AI = IA = A</span>. It is the equivalent of the number 1 for the product, and it serves to define the inverse: <span class="m">A⁻¹A = I</span>.
@@s080 A <b>diagonal</b> matrix has any numbers on the diagonal and 0 outside. It multiplies each component of the vector by the corresponding number, without mixing the components: it is a transformation that stretches or shortens each axis on its own. In NumPy <code>np.eye(n)</code> and <code>np.diag([d1, d2, …])</code>.
@@s081 Ix = x and diag(2, 3) x
@@s082 Orthogonal matrices
@@s083 A square matrix Q is <b>orthogonal</b> (slides 48–49) if all its columns have <b>length 1</b> and are <b>perpendicular</b> two by two. It is written in a single formula: <span class="m">QᵀQ = I</span>, because the element (i, j) of QᵀQ is the inner product between columns i and j.
@@s084 Two consequences. The inverse costs nothing: <span class="m">Q⁻¹ = Qᵀ</span>. And Q does <b>not distort</b>: it preserves lengths and angles, ‖Qv‖ = ‖v‖. Orthogonal matrices are the rotations and reflections.
@@s085 Where they come back: the orthonormal bases of week 6, and the PCA of week 12, where the eigenvectors of the covariance form an orthogonal matrix that "rotates" the data onto the principal axes.
@@s086 Q rotates by 90° and does not stretch
